from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate,Paragraph,Spacer,Table,TableStyle
from reportlab.lib.styles import getSampleStyleSheet
from reportlab.lib import colors
import csv
rows=list(csv.DictReader(open('/mnt/data/modal_frame_results.csv')))
path='/mnt/data/modal_frame_extension.pdf'
doc=SimpleDocTemplate(path,pagesize=(595,842),rightMargin=55,leftMargin=55,topMargin=58,bottomMargin=55)
s=getSampleStyleSheet();story=[]
def p(t,style='BodyText'):story.append(Paragraph(t,s[style]));story.append(Spacer(1,10))
p('Validated Collective–Disagreement Coordinates for Nonlinear Oscillator Resource Bounds','Title')
p('Research supplement | November 2026','Normal')
p('Abstract. A fixed linear coordinate change separates the collective and disagreement variables of a real two-oscillator Stuart–Landau subsystem. Exact-rational interval integration with dyadic outward rounding and Picard step enclosures is compared with direct physical-coordinate interval integration. The comparison measures global coordinate hull widths for the same initial uncertain set, rather than per-cell widths.')
p('Model and enclosure','Heading2')
p('The system is u\u2032 = au - u<super>3</super> - k(u-v), v\u2032 = av - v<super>3</super> - k(v-u), and J\u2032 = 2c<sub>A</sub>k<super>2</super>(u-v)<super>2</super>. With p=(u+v)/2 and m=(u-v)/2, its exact transformed dynamics are p\u2032 = ap-p<super>3</super>-3pm<super>2</super>, m\u2032 = (a-2k)m-3p<super>2</super>m-m<super>3</super>, and J\u2032 = 8c<sub>A</sub>k<super>2</super>m<super>2</super>. The parameter a remains fixed but unknown in [0.15,0.19].')
p('Both methods start from the same correlated initial set p in [0.329,0.331], m in [0.119,0.121], J=0. The direct method begins with its physical-coordinate interval hull. The modal method retains the original parallelogram description in p,m. Thus the modal method preserves an initial correlation lost by the physical-coordinate hull; the comparison does not isolate time-stepping wrapping alone. Coupling k=0.7, actuator coefficient c<sub>A</sub>=0.1, step size h=0.01, and dyadic precision 40 bits.')
p('Each step constructs an interval Y containing X and checks X+h f(Y) is contained in Y, which establishes the local Picard enclosure for the polynomial vector field. The endpoint enclosure is outward-rounded X+h f(Y). This is a first-order interval method with validated continuous-time containment, not a high-order Taylor or adaptive Lohner integrator.')
p('Results','Heading2')
data=[['H','Direct width','Modal width','Direct J upper','Modal J upper']]
for r in rows[1:]:data.append([f"{float(r['horizon']):.1f}",f"{float(r['direct_state_width']):.6f}",f"{float(r['modal_frame_state_width']):.6f}",f"{float(r['direct_J_upper']):.6f}",f"{float(r['modal_J_upper']):.6f}"])
t=Table(data,colWidths=[40,106,106,106,106],repeatRows=1);t.setStyle(TableStyle([('BACKGROUND',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.HexColor('#e5e8ed')),('GRID',(0,0),(-1,-1),.3,colors.grey),('FONTSIZE',(0,0),(-1,-1),9),('BOTTOMPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),7),('TOPPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),7)]));story.append(t);story.append(Spacer(1,12))
p('Resource-floor check','Heading2')
p('For E\u2032=q-b-J\u2032, E(0)=0.1, q=0.01, b=0.005, and floor 0.08, a sufficient all-time lower bound over [0,H] is E(t) at least 0.1 - 0.005H - J<sub>upper</sub>(H), since J is nondecreasing and supply is nonnegative. At H=0.8 the modal certificate gives 0.0939773, above the floor. This bound intentionally discards positive supply and is conservative.')
p('Scope and limitations','Heading2')
p('The result is a continuous-time validated interval calculation for a reduced real two-oscillator system, with exact rational interval endpoints. It is not a moving affine frame, does not establish a full complex Stuart–Landau network theorem, and does not establish calibration to biological energy consumption. The advantage includes preservation of the correlated initial uncertainty. Future work should compare against a physical-coordinate affine enclosure retaining the same initial correlation, and extend the validated coordinate transformation to complex networks and uncertain parameters.')
p('Reproducibility: modal_frame_validated.py; modal_frame_results.csv. All numerical values are generated by the accompanying script.')
doc.build(story)
print(path)
